13
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:08,480
But first, let's take a quick step back and let's talk about WebRTC.

14
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:10,560
So, what is WebRTC?

15
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:20,400
Well, WebRTC is a peer-to-peer network connection that allows you to communicate with your players inside of a game peer-to-peer.

16
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:22,628
So there's no server involved.

17
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:25,040
Or at least that's what most people say.

18
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:30,480
To be able to start with WebRTC, you actually need what's called a signaling server.

19
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:36,640
Now, a signaling server is the place where all of your information is held in the beginning.

20
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:44,240
So think of it kind of like if this player wants to host a lobby, they need to talk to the signaling server to host that lobby.

21
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:48,480
It is your dedicated server that holds on to your user information.

22
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:53,200
It holds on to what lobbies are open, how many people are in those lobbies, things like that.

23
00:01:53,360 --> 00:02:00,800
And it's also the thing that does the actual signaling of the data between your different peers.

24
00:02:01,006 --> 00:02:03,760
So you might be asking, okay, so how does it happen?

25
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:13,840
Well, when a user like this guy tells this guy, hey, I want to make a lobby, they create a lobby and the signaling server keeps track of like a GUID, basically.

26
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,680
And that GUID gets passed back to this guy.

27
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:27,280
And if this guy wants to join that lobby, this guy can send this guy that GUID or that string of information, and then they can use that information to join this lobby.

28
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:35,200
Now, this is all not technically required, but I'm going to go with it as if it is required, just so you guys can kind of get an idea.

29
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:46,560
Once that happens and this player says, Hey, I'm ready to start the game, it sends out what's called a candidate, and that is basically saying, Hey, I'm ready to start playing, let's get moving, right?

30
00:02:46,766 --> 00:02:48,720
So it sends out a candidate to the signaling server.

31
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:51,440
The signaling server is going to send it to this guy.

32
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:54,960
They're going to do what's called an offer, which is going to send back here.

33
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:59,840
It's going to send this guy, and then it's going to give back an answer to your signaling server.

34
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:02,400
And the signaling server is going to relay it to this guy.

35
00:03:02,510 --> 00:03:11,920
And then that's going to create all of the necessary information that they need so that way they can talk to their stun server or their ice server.

36
00:03:12,110 --> 00:03:12,400
Okay.

37
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:15,520
And you might be going, okay, but what is an ice server?

38
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:16,560
What is a stun server?

39
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:21,200
So a stun server's job is to do what's called the NAT connection.

40
00:03:21,422 --> 00:03:32,880
So what it does is it provides the necessary information between these two guys to create a port opening between your routers so that these guys can have a direct communication.

41
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:37,760
Once that direct communication is established, the signaling server is no longer needed.

42
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:43,280
The communication with that is not needed anymore, and the stun server is no longer needed.

43
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:56,000
So once the system, once these two guys have verified that that NAT connection or network address translation connection has been made, then these guys have nothing to do with the connection anymore.

44
00:03:56,080 --> 00:03:58,240
And these guys are peer to peer connected.

45
00:03:58,382 --> 00:04:02,660
Now you might be asking, well, okay, but what happens if that peer to peer connection is not made?

46
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:03,200
Right?

47
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:05,668
What if something breaks along the way?

48
00:04:06,286 --> 00:04:09,040
Well, that's where the turn server comes in.

49
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:15,520
Now, in this case, in this diagram, the turn server is the stun server, so it's the same server, right?

50
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:17,120
It's the same ICE server.

51
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:19,280
But it doesn't have to be.

52
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:20,080
It could be anything.

53
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:21,920
It could be a second machine that does it.

54
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:30,916
But what it does is it's a machine, much like a direct hosted machine that relays the data between these two guys.

55
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:37,440
So that's basically how it works: is this guy connects up to the signaling server, this guy connects up to the signaling server.

56
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:38,800
They exchange some data.

57
00:04:38,926 --> 00:04:46,320
They then tell the stun server here's the IP address, here's the port, here's all the information that I need to be able to connect to this guy.

58
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:49,040
And then this guy creates a connection between these two.

59
00:04:49,134 --> 00:04:51,520
And that's basically how WebRTC works.

